ANIHCDgAAAIAmUNgBAAAA0AQKOwAA\nAACaQGEHAAAAQBMo7AAAAABo4l/Jlmr5ljjJjAAAAABJRU5ErkJggg=="},"metadata":{"image/png":{"width":420,"height":420}}}],"execution_count":16},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Q3.3: Compare the strength of these correlations. Which temperature metric is most strongly associated with CO2? Why might that be?<br>\nQ3.4: Briefly explain the conceptual difference between Pearson correlation and Spearman's Rank Correlation. In what scenario might Spearman's correlation be more appropriate for analyzing the relationship between CO2 and temperature, even if they appear broadly linearly related?** <br>\n*Bonus: explore correlation calculation of monotonic values. (Kendall's tau relation)*\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"3.3\n\nMy analysis of the data shows a strong correlation between atmospheric CO2 concentrations and global temperatures. We found that land average temperature has a correlation coefficient (r) of 0.903, while the correlation for land-ocean average temperature is even stronger at r=0.917. This is because the oceans' slow warming rate helps to smooth out short-term fluctuations, giving us a clearer and more stable global warming trend.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"3.4\n\nBoth Pearson and Spearman correlations analyze the relationship between variables. While Pearson works best for linear relationships in interval or ratio data, Spearman is more flexible, using ranks to measure any monotonic relationship. For our CO2 and temperature analysis, Spearman's correlation could have been a better choice if the relationship wasn't perfectly linear or if we needed a measure less influenced by outliers.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Section 4: Hidden Variable and Causation","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Q4.1: While our analysis shows a strong correlation between Yearly_CO2_Concentration and GlobalAverageTemperature, can we definitively say that increasing CO2 causes the temperature to rise based solely on this correlation? Explain your reasoning and discuss why correlation does not imply causation. <br><br>\nQ4.2: Beyond CO2, what other factors or \"hidden variables\" might influence global average temperatures? Briefly discuss how these factors could potentially make the relationship between CO2 and temperature seem more or less strong if not considered.**\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"I agree. It is crucial to remember that correlation does not prove causation. While our analysis shows a strong statistical association between higher CO2 levels and increased global temperatures, it does not explain the underlying reason. Other scientific factors, such as solar activity and the influence of other greenhouse gases, could also play a role. Ultimately, correlation is a powerful tool for identifying relationships, but it only gives us a statistical link—not the definitive cause behind it.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Section 5: Missing Data Mechanisms","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Q5.1 From the dataset snippet identify missing data as Missing Completely At Random (MCAR), Missing At Random (MAR) and Missing Not At Random (MNAR). Justify** <br>\n*Hint: read up about ozone sensors to answer this question*","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"data <- read.csv(\"/kaggle/input/missing-data/missing_data.csv\")\nhead(data)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2025-09-04T16:53:05.949805Z","iopub.execute_input":"2025-09-04T16:53:05.951538Z","iopub.status.idle":"2025-09-04T16:53:05.983937Z","shell.execute_reply":"2025-09-04T16:53:05.981948Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/html":"<table class=\"dataframe\">\n<caption>A data.frame: 5 × 4</caption>\n<thead>\n\t<tr><th></th><th scope=col>Station_ID</th><th scope=col>Location_Type</th><th scope=col>Humidity....</th><th scope=col>Ozone_Level..ppb.</th></tr>\n\t<tr><th>